\subsection{Reserve Output Credits before Accepting a Record}

Checking a single span does not prevent a whole series of valid
records from filling the host queue. Give each instance a logical
byte quota and a record quota. The latter bounds per-record overhead
and also accounts for zero-length records. Assume a synchronous bounded
snapshot of the caller's record is made in fixed-size scratch storage
after span and UTF-8 checks; scratch is budgeted separately. A sink
lock serializes admission and accounting, and a bounded queue supports
all-or-none append. Successful append admits the immutable record for
delivery, while rejection has no output effect. The policy below
counts a record even after a worker takes it from the queue.

\begin{sudocode}[Account for both queued and in-flight records]
\State $r\gets$ validated snapshot of caller's record, or reject
\State acquire sink lock
\If{$|r|>freeBytes$ or $freeRecords=0$}
  \State unlock and return busy \Comment{Do not wait inside the import}
\EndIf
\If{appending an owned copy of $r$ fails}
  \State unlock and return busy \Comment{No credits consumed}
\EndIf
\State $freeBytes\gets freeBytes-|r|$
\State $freeRecords\gets freeRecords-1$
\State unlock and return accepted
\State on terminal delivery or discard, refund both under lock
\end{sudocode}

With six byte credits and two record credits, accept a four-byte
record and an empty one. A two-byte record is then refused even
though two byte credits remain. Move the four-byte record into
delivery but pause the sink; the third call is still refused.
Once that delivery finishes, the two-byte record may be accepted.
The trace must contain one delivery of each accepted record,
including the empty one, assuming the sink finishes successfully.
Give a second instance its own quotas and verify the refusal on
the first does not consume any of the second's allocation.

The invariant is that remaining credits plus charges for all owned
outstanding records equal the initial quotas. Refund once, after
terminal handling, not at dequeue. This primitive bounds one sink's
retained data; a runtime admitting many instances must separately
bound aggregate storage and schedule delivery fairly.

\section{What must travel with code}

In \emph{Analyzing Mobile Code Languages}, Gianpaolo Cugola, Carlo Ghezzi, Gian Pietro Picco, and Giovanni Vigna distinguish moving code from moving its execution state and data bindings. A portable module can be instantiated anew; a portable suspended instance needs a representation of the continuation and policy for rebinding external resources. Do not serialize a native pointer or assume the destination has equivalent authority. Revalidate imports at the destination and reject continuation state or features that the destination cannot represent. The Modern Compiler Dossier's Chapter~34, \emph{Capability-Secured Compiler IR}, applies a similar idea at another layer: a pass needs explicit authority for its actions even when it operates on well-typed IR.
